---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import FAQ from '../../components/FAQ.astro';
import MembershipUpgradeCalculator from '../../components/MembershipUpgradeCalculator.astro';
import { localizedPath } from '../../i18n/utils';
import { costcoUs, percent, perMonth, savingsBreakeven, usd } from '../../lib/rates';

const locale = 'en';
const gold = costcoUs.tiers.gold_star;
const executive = costcoUs.tiers.executive;
const rewardRate = executive.reward_rate;
const delta = costcoUs.upgrade_delta;
const breakeven = costcoUs.breakeven_qualified_spend;
const monthly = perMonth(breakeven);
// 文章裡用到的每個情境值，都由門檻推導而不是各自寫死。
const belowThreshold = 1800;
const familyMonthly = 400;
const businessMonthly = 800;
const nearMiss = breakeven + 250;
const comfortable = breakeven + 750;
const highSpend = breakeven * 2;
const wholeFeeSpend = executive.annual_fee / rewardRate;
const basicAt5 = savingsBreakeven(gold.annual_fee, 0.05);
const basicAt10 = savingsBreakeven(gold.annual_fee, 0.10);
const faq = [
  {
    question: 'How much do I need to spend for Costco Executive to be worth it?',
    answer:
      `Using the ${usd(delta)} difference between a ${usd(executive.annual_fee)} ${executive.name} fee and a ${usd(gold.annual_fee)} ${gold.name} fee, the ${percent(rewardRate)} reward must apply to ${usd(breakeven)} of annual eligible spending: ${usd(delta)} divided by ${rewardRate}. That is about ${usd(monthly, 2)} per month. Verify current fees and reward eligibility before upgrading.`,
  },
  {
    question: `Does ${usd(breakeven)} make ${executive.name} free?`,
    answer:
      `No. At ${usd(breakeven)} of eligible spending, the estimated ${usd(delta)} reward only recovers the extra ${usd(delta)} paid to upgrade from ${gold.name}. The underlying ${usd(gold.annual_fee)} membership cost must still be justified by store savings or other value you actually use.`,
  },
  {
    question: 'Does every Costco purchase earn the 2% reward?',
    answer:
      'Do not assume so. Reward rules, excluded categories, caps, taxes, fees, and timing can affect eligible spending. Use only purchases you reasonably expect to qualify and check Costco\'s current terms for the authoritative rules.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is Costco Executive worth it for one person?',
    answer:
      `Household size is less important than eligible annual spending. A single shopper below ${usd(breakeven)} a year will generally earn less than the ${usd(delta)} upgrade difference from the ${percent(rewardRate)} reward alone, while a high-spending single shopper can cross the threshold.`,
  },
  {
    question: 'Can a small business justify an Executive membership?',
    answer:
      'Possibly. Repeated eligible purchases can make the threshold easier to reach, but only net spending that remains after returns should be counted. Separate business convenience from the reward math and confirm that the categories you buy qualify.',
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
  title={`Is Costco ${executive.name} Worth It? ${usd(breakeven)} Break-Even Math`}
  description={`Costco ${executive.name} beats ${gold.name} at ${usd(breakeven)} of eligible annual spend: verify the ${usd(delta)} upgrade, ${percent(rewardRate)} exclusions, safety margin, and your own result.`}
  locale={locale}
  alternateLocales={['en', 'zh', 'es', 'fr', 'de']}
  updated={costcoUs.checked}
>
  <p class="direct-answer">
    <strong>Short answer: Costco {executive.name} is mathematically better than {gold.name} once you have {usd(breakeven)} in eligible annual spending, or roughly {usd(monthly, 2)} per month.</strong>
    That threshold follows directly from a {usd(delta)} upgrade difference divided by a {percent(rewardRate)} reward. It is not a promise that every purchase qualifies, and it does not prove that either membership is worthwhile for you. For a personalized result, use the
    <a href={localizedPath(locale, '/tools/costco-membership')}>Costco membership calculator</a>
    to enter your monthly spend, expected price savings, and reward rate.
  </p>

  <h2>Gold Star vs. Executive at a glance</h2>
  <p>
    The calculator models {gold.name} at {usd(gold.annual_fee)} per year and {executive.name} at {usd(executive.annual_fee)} per year. Both tiers provide the basic ability to shop at Costco. In the model, {executive.name} adds a {percent(rewardRate)} reward on eligible spending. Because the base shopping access is shared, the clean upgrade decision compares only two incremental numbers: the extra {usd(delta)} annual fee and the reward produced by eligible purchases.
  </p>
  <p>
    That distinction matters. Asking whether a Costco membership is worth it is different from asking whether Executive is worth more than Gold Star. The first question compares the full membership fee with savings you receive relative to your normal stores. The second compares the extra Executive fee with the extra reward. Mixing the two questions can make an upgrade look better than it really is.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>{gold.name}:</strong> {usd(gold.annual_fee)} annual fee in the calculator; no {percent(rewardRate)} {executive.name} reward.</li>
    <li><strong>{executive.name}:</strong> {usd(executive.annual_fee)} annual fee in the calculator; modeled with a {percent(rewardRate)} reward on eligible spending.</li>
    <li><strong>Upgrade difference:</strong> {usd(executive.annual_fee)} minus {usd(gold.annual_fee)} equals {usd(delta)} per year.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Fees and program terms can change. Treat those values as the inputs used for this analysis, then verify current prices, eligible categories, exclusions, reward timing, and caps with Costco before purchasing or renewing.
  </p>

  <MembershipUpgradeCalculator
    locale="en-US"
    currency="USD"
    extraFee={delta}
    monthlySpend={300}
    rewardRate={rewardRate * 100}
    copy={{
      heading: 'Executive upgrade break-even calculator', intro: 'Use eligible net purchases only. Exclude returns, taxes, fees and categories the current Executive terms do not reward.',
      extraFee: 'Annual upgrade difference', extraFeeHelp: 'Executive fee minus the base membership you would otherwise renew.', monthlySpend: 'Eligible monthly spend', monthlySpendHelp: 'A normal-year estimate after returns and exclusions.',
      rewardRate: 'Incremental reward rate (%)', rewardRateHelp: 'The current published Executive rate is 2%; terms can change.', extraBenefits: 'Other measurable annual value', extraBenefitsHelp: 'Only include upgrade-only benefits you would otherwise pay for.',
      verdictLabel: 'Upgrade result', annualReward: 'Estimated annual reward', netValue: 'Net after upgrade fee', breakEven: 'Eligible monthly break-even', favorable: 'The upgrade is ahead by {net} per year under these inputs. Confirm current eligibility and timing.', unfavorable: 'The upgrade is behind by {net} per year. It needs about {threshold} in eligible monthly spend to break even.',
    }}
  />

  <h2>The 2% reward break-even calculation</h2>
  <p>
    The break-even formula is straightforward: divide the additional annual fee by the reward rate. Written out, it is <strong>{usd(delta)} / {rewardRate} = {usd(breakeven)}</strong> in eligible annual spending. Dividing by 12 gives about <strong>{usd(monthly, 2)} per month</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    At exactly {usd(breakeven)} of eligible spending, a {percent(rewardRate)} reward is {usd(delta)}. That estimated reward offsets the extra {usd(delta)} paid for {executive.name}, leaving you economically even with {gold.name} on the upgrade itself. At {usd(comfortable)} of eligible spending, the reward is {usd(comfortable * rewardRate)}, so {executive.name} is ahead by {usd(comfortable * rewardRate - delta)} before considering any differences not captured in this simple model. At {usd(highSpend)}, the reward is {usd(highSpend * rewardRate)}, putting {executive.name} {usd(highSpend * rewardRate - delta)} ahead of {gold.name} on reward minus upgrade fee.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>$2,000 eligible spend x 2% = $40 reward; Executive trails Gold Star by $25.</li>
    <li>{usd(breakeven)} eligible spend x {percent(rewardRate)} = {usd(delta)} reward; the upgrade breaks even.</li>
    <li>$4,000 eligible spend x 2% = $80 reward; Executive leads by $15.</li>
    <li>$6,000 eligible spend x 2% = $120 reward; Executive leads by $55.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The margin above the threshold is more useful than the threshold alone. If your estimate is {usd(nearMiss)}, a small amount of ineligible spending or a return can erase the projected advantage. If your conservative eligible-spend estimate is far above {usd(breakeven)}, the decision is more robust.
  </p>

  <h2>Why total Costco spending is not always eligible spending</h2>
  <p>
    The formula works only when its spending input is honest. Your card statement or warehouse receipts may include taxes, fees, returns, or categories that do not earn the reward under current program rules. Do not multiply every dollar connected to a Costco trip by 2% without checking eligibility. Use the portion you reasonably expect to qualify after likely returns.
  </p>
  <p>
    Timing also matters near renewal. A household can spend {usd(breakeven)} over a calendar year but have a different amount fall within its membership reward period. Rather than trying to engineer a result to the dollar, estimate a normal full year and add a safety margin. The official membership terms are the source of truth; this article explains the arithmetic, not program administration.
  </p>

  <h2>Do not confuse the upgrade break-even with the membership break-even</h2>
  <p>
    The {usd(breakeven)} threshold answers one narrow question: when does the {percent(rewardRate)} reward recover the extra {usd(delta)} for {executive.name}? It does not recover the entire {usd(executive.annual_fee)} {executive.name} fee by itself. A {percent(rewardRate)} reward would need {usd(wholeFeeSpend)} of eligible spending to equal {usd(executive.annual_fee)}, but that is usually the wrong comparison because {gold.name} already costs {usd(gold.annual_fee)} and provides the underlying membership access.
  </p>
  <p>
    To decide whether membership access itself pays off, estimate how much Costco saves you on a weighted, like-for-like basket of purchases you would make anyway. Let <em>s</em> be that measured savings rate and <em>C</em> the annual travel, storage and waste cost. The base membership's monthly break-even is <strong>({usd(gold.annual_fee)} + C) ÷ (12 × s)</strong>. There is no honest store-wide percentage that applies to every household.
  </p>
  <p>
    For example, if your comparable basket is 5% cheaper and there are no extra costs, {gold.name} would require {usd(basicAt5)} of annual spending to recover a {usd(gold.annual_fee)} fee. At 10%, that threshold is {usd(basicAt10)}. If bulk waste or impulse purchases consume the price difference, your real savings rate can be zero or negative. Use the
    <a href={localizedPath(locale, '/tools/costco-membership')}>calculator with your own savings assumption</a>
    instead of treating the default as a fact.
  </p>

  <h2>Scenario 1: a single shopper</h2>
  <p>
    Consider a single shopper who spends {usd(belowThreshold / 12, 2)} per month on eligible Costco purchases. Annual eligible spend is {usd(belowThreshold)}, and {percent(rewardRate)} of that is {usd(belowThreshold * rewardRate)}. Against the {usd(delta)} upgrade difference, {executive.name} is behind {gold.name} by {usd(delta - belowThreshold * rewardRate)}. The person may still justify Gold Star through genuine price savings, convenient fuel stops, or products they already buy, but the reward alone does not support upgrading.
  </p>
  <p>
    A single person can still cross the Executive threshold. Household size is only a rough proxy for spending. Someone buying recurring staples, optical products, or supplies that qualify under current rules could spend more than a larger household. The disciplined approach is to review a representative year of purchases, remove questionable categories, and calculate 2% of the remainder.
  </p>

  <h2>Scenario 2: a family</h2>
  <p>
    Suppose a family expects {usd(familyMonthly)} per month of eligible spending. That is {usd(familyMonthly * 12)} per year and an estimated {usd(familyMonthly * 12 * rewardRate)} reward. Subtract the {usd(delta)} upgrade difference and {executive.name} is ahead by {usd(familyMonthly * 12 * rewardRate - delta)}. This is a positive result, but it is not so large that the family should ignore waste, returns, or eligibility.
  </p>
  <p>
    Families often have more opportunities to use bulk quantities before they spoil, but that is not guaranteed. Buying a larger package only saves money if it replaces a purchase the household would have made elsewhere and the product is actually used. The reward should never be a reason to spend an extra dollar: at 2%, spending $100 more produces only $2 of reward while still costing $98 net.
  </p>

  <h2>Scenario 3: a small business</h2>
  <p>
    A small business buying {usd(businessMonthly)} per month of eligible supplies would have {usd(businessMonthly * 12)} of annual eligible spend. At {percent(rewardRate)}, the estimated reward is {usd(businessMonthly * 12 * rewardRate)}. After the {usd(delta)} upgrade difference, {executive.name} is ahead by {usd(businessMonthly * 12 * rewardRate - delta)}. That is a clearer mathematical margin, assuming those purchases qualify and remain after returns.
  </p>
  <p>
    Business owners should keep the reward decision separate from tax treatment and bookkeeping. This calculator does not determine deductibility, substantiation, or how a reward should be recorded. Those questions depend on facts and applicable tax rules; use proper records and consult a qualified professional when needed.
  </p>

  <h2>Five checks before you upgrade</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Use eligible net spending.</strong> Exclude purchases that do not qualify and subtract expected returns.</li>
    <li><strong>Use a full, normal year.</strong> Do not annualize an unusually expensive holiday month or one-time purchase without adjustment.</li>
    <li><strong>Demand a margin of safety.</strong> A forecast barely above {usd(breakeven)} is effectively a tie because spending and rules can change.</li>
    <li><strong>Ignore reward-driven spending.</strong> Buying more to earn 2% makes you poorer unless the purchase was already needed and competitively priced.</li>
    <li><strong>Verify current terms.</strong> Confirm fees, reward percentage, eligible categories, exclusions, cap, and redemption rules directly with Costco.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>A practical decision rule</h2>
  <p>
    Choose {gold.name} when your conservative estimate of eligible annual spending is below {usd(breakeven)} and no separate {executive.name} benefit has measurable value to you. Consider {executive.name} when that estimate is comfortably above {usd(breakeven)}. If you are close, the expected difference is small: at {usd(nearMiss)} of eligible spending, the reward is {usd(nearMiss * rewardRate)}, only {usd(nearMiss * rewardRate - delta)} more than the upgrade difference. Convenience and uncertainty matter more than a projected five-dollar edge.
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, calculate the membership and the upgrade as two separate decisions. First ask whether realistic store savings recover at least the base fee. Then ask whether the 2% reward recovers the extra Executive fee. The
    <a href={localizedPath(locale, '/tools/costco-membership')}>free Costco membership calculator</a>
    performs both comparisons with your spending and assumptions, entirely in your browser.
  </p>

  <h2>Official sources</h2>
  <ul class="source-list">
    <li><a href="https://www.costco.com/f/-/join-costco">Costco US — current Gold Star and Executive fees and benefits</a></li>
    <li><a href="https://www.costco.com/f/-/executive-rewards">Costco US — Executive 2% reward, cap, calculation and exclusions</a></li>
  </ul>
  <p class="last-verified"><small>Official terms last checked July 19, 2026. Fees, benefits, caps, qualifying purchases and redemption rules can change; verify them before upgrading or renewing.</small></p>

  <FAQ items={faq} />

  <p class="privacy-note">
    This breakdown is an educational estimate, not financial or tax advice. The fees and 2% rate shown are calculator inputs used for transparent mathematical examples. Verify current Costco pricing and official reward terms before making a membership decision.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
